Stay Safe Online: A Cyber Safety Checklist for College Students
Managing your digital life is a key part of college, but it also exposes you to various cyber risks. This checklist will help you review your online habits and stay secure from common threats in India.

🛡️ Basic Digital Safety Practices

Turn on Two-Factor Authentication (2FA): Enable 2FA on your university email, messaging apps like WhatsApp, and social media accounts. Whenever possible, use an authenticator app instead of SMS-based OTPs for better security.
Review App Permissions Regularly: Check your phone settings and remove access to features like the camera, microphone, or contacts for apps that don’t truly require them.
Create Strong and Unique Passwords: Avoid predictable passwords such as your name or date of birth. Use long passphrases or a password manager to keep each account secure with a different password.
Be Careful with Public Wi-Fi: Avoid logging into sensitive accounts or making financial transactions on open or hostel networks. If necessary, use a trusted VPN connection.
Think Before Clicking Links: Be cautious of shortened or suspicious links in emails related to jobs or exam results. Always preview the actual URL before opening it.
Protect Your Webcam: Cover your laptop camera with a shutter or tape when not in use to prevent unauthorized access by malware.
💸 UPI and Financial Safety Tips
Your PIN is Only for Sending Money: Never enter your UPI PIN to receive funds. If someone asks you to do so for a reward, refund, or prize, it is a scam.
Check the Receiver’s Name Carefully: Before confirming a payment, verify the displayed merchant or recipient name, as scammers often use similar-looking names to trick users.
🚨 What to Do If You Face Fraud
If you become a victim of cybercrime or financial fraud, act immediately. Report the issue through official Government of India channels such as the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal or call the national helpline at 1930, which operates круглосуточно.

Stay aware. Stay protected.

